{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1786510217,"nonce":"f6/gTLT9CZN38MU4MY7d8i9eYBM=","title":"Title: `alert_event_keys` grows forever: its own index and table comment name a 30-day pruner that does not exist","message":"**What is wrong.** The schema ships the pruner's index and the pruner's documentation, and no pruner. `db/migrations/00007_alerts.sql:298` creates `alert_event_keys_prune_idx ON alert_event_keys (created_at)` directly under the comment *\"Serves: the 30-day pruner. The idempotency probe itself rides the PRIMARY KEY.\"* — an index that exists for exactly one query, and that query has never been written. The table comment at `:301` states it as fact: *\"Pruned at created_at \u003c now() - 30 days.\"*\n\n`internal/app/workers.go:334-352` is `retention.prune`, the only row-level retention job in oto. It prunes `ingest_dedup` (`:337`), `sessions` (`:341`) and delivery drills (`:345`), and its own doc comment at `:328-333` describes its remit as \"the deliberately UNPARTITIONED idempotency siblings and the session table\". `alert_event_keys` is the other unpartitioned idempotency sibling and it is not there. Repo-wide there are nine non-test `DELETE FROM` statements and none of them names this table.\n\n`docs/adr/0024-retention-defaults-and-cold-storage.md:212-215` already names the defect and leaves it open: *\"Nothing prunes `alert_event_keys`. SPEC §D.4 and its own table comment promise 30 days; `pruneRetention` prunes only `ingest_dedup` and `sessions`. The table grows forever.\"*\n\n**The demonstration.** Two writers insert into it. `internal/alerts/repository/event.go:117-121` claims one row per keyed event in a batch; `internal/notification/repository/events.go:73-76` does the same for one timeline row. Only events carrying a `dedupe_key` claim a row (`event.go:170-187` splits keyed from unkeyed), but every lifecycle transition carries one — `internal/alerts/domain/event.go:198` gives the shape, `occ:{occurrence_id}:opened` — so in practice it is one row per state transition.\n\n`alert_events` is reclaimed by monthly partition drops at `event_retention_months`, default 13. `alert_event_keys` is on ADR 0024's list at `:39`: **\"Never reaped, by anything, at any setting.\"** The key table therefore outlives every event it indexes.\n\nAt ADR 0024's own volume model — ~8 events per firing episode (`:92`) at 10 000 firings a day (`:247`) — that is ~80 000 rows a day, ~29M rows in the first year, and no year in which it stops. The only query the table serves is the `INSERT … ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING` probe at `event.go:117-121`, which runs inside the ingest transaction. The index that grows without bound is the one on the hot path.\n\n**The second consequence: SPEC acceptance criterion 36 would break, and break silently.** AC-36 (`docs/design/SPEC.md:3840`) is *\"replaying a stored `ingest_batch` after a parser fix reproduces the same state without duplicate Slack messages\"*, and it is the one stated requirement a stored raw payload exists to serve (`internal/platform/tuning/defaults.go:143-150`). A replay re-appends events under the same dedupe keys; `AppendBatch` finds the keys still claimed, skips the `alert_events` insert, and returns zero written — which `event.go:139-141` documents as \"the idempotency mechanism working, not an error\", so nothing surfaces. While the events still exist that is correct behaviour. It stops being correct the moment the keys outlive the events they point at: the replay restores nothing and reports success.\n\nThat configuration is reachable today, not hypothetically. `internal/identity/domain/settings.go:209` permits `raw_retention_days` up to 365; `:214` permits `event_retention_months` down to 1. An operator on 365/1 holds replayable payloads for a year whose event partitions were dropped eleven months earlier, and every replay of them would be a permanent no-op. No batch-replay entry point is shipped yet, which is the only reason this is \"would\" rather than \"does\" — the hazard is baked in ahead of the feature, and the surviving keys are what bakes it in.\n\n**Why it matters.** Five places derive the shipped `raw_retention_days = 30` from this pruner's horizon and state that the two numbers move together: `docs/design/SPEC.md:953`, `docs/adr/0024-...:112-121`, `internal/platform/tuning/defaults.go:139-154`, `db/migrations/00036_retention_defaults.sql:8-13`, and `internal/identity/domain/settings.go:206-210`. A derived constant is currently derived from a horizon that is infinite. Fixing the pruner is what makes those five statements true; leaving it is what makes the growth permanent.\n\n**Where.** `internal/app/workers.go:334-352` (`pruneRetention`, the missing branch), `db/migrations/00007_alerts.sql:298` and `:301` (the index and comment that promise it), `internal/alerts/repository/event.go:117-121` and `internal/notification/repository/events.go:73-76` (the two writers), `docs/adr/0024-retention-defaults-and-cold-storage.md:212-215`.\n\n**Scope.** small\n\n**Done when.** `pruneRetention` deletes `alert_event_keys` rows older than the effective raw-retention window its sibling `partitions.manage` already computes (`Container.effectiveRetention`, `internal/app/workers.go:295-326`), bounded per tick by `sweepLimit` (`:142`), and the count is logged alongside `ingest_dedup` and `sessions`. The table comment at `db/migrations/00007_alerts.sql:301` becomes a true statement, and ADR 0024's open defect 1 can be struck.","files":null}]}